                              WHY IT EXISTS

A Strata program is compiled by pasting the runtime support code in
`compiler/runtime_preamble.c` — and any standard library module the program
imports — into the C that is generated for it. That code therefore ends up
inside every binary the compiler produces.

Without this exception, a program compiled with Strata would contain code
covered by the License, and whoever shipped that program would inherit the
License's attribution and notice obligations for it — even though they wrote
none of it and may not be distributing Strata at all.

That is not the intent. The compiler is Apache-2.0 so that contributions to
Strata carry an express patent grant. What somebody builds WITH Strata is
theirs.

The same problem is solved the same way by the Swift and LLVM projects, whose
runtime library exceptions this one follows in intent.


